faqTopic: trauma
faq:
  - q: How do I know whether what I went through counts as trauma?
    a: You do not need to know that beforehand. Whether an event is called trauma depends less on the event itself than on the mark it left on you. "Other people have been through worse" is something we hear very often, and it is not a sufficient reason to stay away.
  - q: Do I have to tell the whole story?
    a: No. You decide how much to say and when. Pace matters in trauma work; you will not be pushed to describe something you are not ready to describe. The process moves at a speed you can carry.
  - q: Do you offer EMDR?
    a: No, EMDR is not offered at our centre. Trauma-focused work here is carried out through cognitive behavioural, psychodynamic and acceptance and commitment approaches, and through play and art-based methods with children.
  - q: Does my child have to talk about what happened?
    a: With younger children the work usually does not run through talking. It proceeds through play, drawing and story. Meetings with parents are a separate and regular part of the process.
  - q: How long does trauma therapy take?
    a: There is no single length that can be given in advance. Work related to a single incident and work related to experiences spread over years do not move at the same pace. After the first meetings, the frame for working together is discussed with you.
