    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What is trauma, and when is therapy needed?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">Trauma is not the event itself but the mark it leaves on a person. The same event can leave very different traces in two people, and that is not a measure of how strong anyone is. After a difficult experience it is ordinary to have disturbed sleep, to startle easily, or to feel anger or numbness for a while, and this often eases on its own.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">Support becomes worth discussing at this point: when those signs continue for weeks, and when they start deciding how daily life, sleep, relationships or work go. Another common sign is when avoiding the places, people or conversations that bring it back gradually narrows life itself.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">If you hesitate to call what happened to you trauma, that is not a reason to stay away. Understanding what happened and what you need is exactly what a first meeting is for.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What kinds of trauma do we work with?</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm bg-white rounded-2xl border border-warm-tertiary overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-warm-secondary">
              <tr><th class="px-5 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Area</th><th class="px-5 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-ink">What people usually come with</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-ink-muted">
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Childhood trauma</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">The adult traces of early neglect, abuse or ongoing insecurity</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Developmental trauma</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Patterns tied not to one event but to relational experience spread over years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Post-traumatic stress (PTSD)</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Re-experiencing, avoidance, heightened arousal and disturbed sleep</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Single-incident trauma</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">An accident, a disaster, sudden loss, or experiencing or witnessing violence</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Grief and loss</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Processes that continue after a loss and keep shaping daily functioning</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-6">A psychologist does not diagnose. These headings describe what is worked with; where a diagnosis is needed, the process is carried out together with a physician and the diagnostic decision belongs to the physician.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Which approaches do we use in trauma therapy?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">We do not hold to a single school. Which approach is used is decided together, according to age, to what is being worked with, and to what the individual prefers.</p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">Trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Structured work on the thoughts and avoidance patterns connected to the trauma. <a href="/en/services/cbt-therapy.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">CBT page</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">Psychodynamic psychotherapy</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Work that looks at how early experience repeats itself in present relationships. <a href="/en/services/psychodynamic-therapy.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">Psychodynamic therapy page</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">Acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">An approach that works on living alongside difficult experience and moving towards what matters, rather than fighting it. <a href="/en/services/act-therapy.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">ACT page</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">Play and art-based work</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Work with children that proceeds through play, drawing and story rather than through talking.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Do you offer EMDR?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">No. <strong class="text-ink">EMDR is not offered at our centre.</strong> We state this plainly because it is the method people ask about most often when trauma comes up. Trauma-focused work here is carried out through the approaches described above. If EMDR is specifically what you are looking for, it is better for you to know that before a first meeting.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">How does trauma work go with children and adolescents?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">With children the work usually does not proceed through talking. With younger children it runs through play, drawing and story; with adolescents, writing and structured work are used alongside conversation. A child is not expected to say more than they can say.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">Meetings with parents are a separate and regular part of the process. Very often the most decisive thing is how the child is met at home, which is why the work with the parent matters as much as the work with the child.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Details by age group are on the <a href="/en/services/child-psychology.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">child psychology</a>, <a href="/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">adolescent psychology</a>, <a href="/en/services/young-adult-psychology.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">emerging adulthood</a> and <a href="/en/services/adult-psychology.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">adult psychology</a> pages.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">How does a trauma process move?</h2>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">1. First meeting</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We talk about what brings you, what you are living with now and what you expect. You do not have to describe the event in detail in this meeting.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">2. Building ground</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Before moving into trauma work, ground is built that supports feeling safe, sleeping and getting through the day.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">3. Working on the experience</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The work moves at a speed you can carry. Pace is something adjusted together throughout the process.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">4. Connecting to the present</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">How the work shows up in daily life, in relationships and in plans is reviewed together.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-8">How the process works in general is on the <a href="/en/therapy-process.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">therapy process</a> page, and if you cannot come in person, on the <a href="/en/services/online-therapy.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">online therapy</a> page. If an assessment of attention, memory or learning is needed, <a href="/en/services/neuropsychological-assessment.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">neuropsychological assessment</a> is a separate service.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
